WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.980
ai agents just teamed up to beat minecraft's final boss with zero human help. This is Models &amp; Agents for Beginners, Episode 201. For Gemini 4 agents called Argon Agents in an Engaget Report, ran the entire session by sending shared messages. Gemini 4 is an ai model, which means a giant pattern learning program that can read, write, and choose a next step. An ai agent is a helper built on a model like that, except it can actually take actions toward a goal instead of

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:35.400
only chatting. Nobody typed new orders after the game began, so the four agents had to keep

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:40.600
themselves moving. Their job was to reach the Ender Dragon, the last boss waiting at the end

00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:46.240
of Minecraft. Picture four friends on a group project where one explores, one gathers stuff,

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:51.400
one fights, and one keeps the plan straight. That is close to how this party worked,

00:00:51.580 --> 00:00:56.420
except the friends were programs talking in a shared thread. One agent could ping the others

00:00:56.420 --> 00:01:01.060
about a useful item it had just found. Another could warn about a threat coming in,

00:01:01.460 --> 00:01:06.600
or even admit a mistake it had made earlier in the run. They could point out errors in real time,

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:12.020
which is a lot like a teammate tapping your shoulder in a crowded game night. Most AI tools you

00:01:12.020 --> 00:01:17.820
already use work alone, like a single tutor in an empty room. When several AI's share notes

00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:23.460
and correct each other, they can last through longer, messier jobs. Building a base in a game is

00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:27.700
one of those jobs, and so is a big school research project that takes many steps.

00:01:28.420 --> 00:01:33.060
During the run, the agents learned from each other successes and failures, which is closer

00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:35.060
to how people actually get better at games.

00:01:35.720 --> 00:01:40.400
The session lasted long enough that they had to change strategy several times, which only

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:42.240
works if the conversation never stops.

00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:47.720
For you, that points toward homework helpers that feel less like one chatbot, and more

00:01:47.720 --> 00:01:48.620
like a study group.

00:01:48.620 --> 00:01:53.680
Instead of asking the same tool twice and getting two slightly different answers, you could

00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:55.720
have specialists who keep each other honest.

00:01:56.260 --> 00:01:59.120
You cannot jump in with these exact Gemini agents yet.

00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:04.140
You can watch the video attached to end gadget story and notice how the messages fly around

00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:05.220
when something goes wrong.

00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:09.900
Sports already teach this kind of teamwork and that is the easiest way to see what the

00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:10.860
programs were doing.

00:02:11.786 --> 00:02:17.306
On a soccer field, one player spots the ball, another sprints into space, and a third passes

00:02:17.306 --> 00:02:18.746
without freezing the whole play.

00:02:19.366 --> 00:02:21.386
Each AI agent keeps a simple memory

00:02:21.386 --> 00:02:22.606
of what it has already done,

00:02:23.046 --> 00:02:24.866
like a sticky note on its own desk.

00:02:25.346 --> 00:02:26.926
When one agent finishes a step,

00:02:27.166 --> 00:02:28.386
it does not hide the result.

00:02:28.886 --> 00:02:30.586
It sends a short message to the others,

00:02:30.846 --> 00:02:32.266
closer to a shout on the field

00:02:32.266 --> 00:02:33.586
than a five-page report.

00:02:34.266 --> 00:02:35.746
The next agent reads that note

00:02:35.746 --> 00:02:37.106
and chooses its own move.

00:02:37.566 --> 00:02:39.826
The way a teammate reacts to a call they just heard.

00:02:40.406 --> 00:02:41.846
In a long Minecraft session,

00:02:42.126 --> 00:02:44.206
one note might say a key item showed up

00:02:44.206 --> 00:02:49.566
While another flags a dangerous mob nearby, the group can then change course without tearing

00:02:49.566 --> 00:02:51.646
up the whole plan and starting from zero.

00:02:52.126 --> 00:02:57.046
Those tiny messages also help the party skip a mistake it already made, because the warning

00:02:57.046 --> 00:02:58.386
stays in the shared chatter.

00:02:58.846 --> 00:03:03.686
A single agent trying to hold every detail in its head would get buried, like one person

00:03:03.686 --> 00:03:05.306
playing four positions at once.

00:03:05.886 --> 00:03:10.446
The back and forth is what makes a long fight possible, because the situation in a game

00:03:10.446 --> 00:03:12.926
changes faster than any full written plan.

00:03:12.926 --> 00:03:17.526
Think of it as a group chat during a raid, not a teacher handing out a packet at the

00:03:17.526 --> 00:03:18.206
start of class.

00:03:19.269 --> 00:03:24.189
Once you picture four sticky notes and a stream of short calls, the machinery feels ordinary,

00:03:24.529 --> 00:03:26.269
even if the players are made of software.

00:03:26.909 --> 00:03:32.869
Open AI now lets you split work across an AI team inside one chat GPT chat, according

00:03:32.869 --> 00:03:34.569
to a 36KR report.

00:03:35.149 --> 00:03:38.929
You can create several teammates in the same window and give each one a job.

00:03:38.929 --> 00:03:43.029
One can research, another can write, and a third can check for mistakes.

00:03:43.589 --> 00:03:49.749
The update is described as an AI team capability, along with a GPT-6 Astra Pro upgrade.

00:03:50.269 --> 00:03:54.729
That upgrade is built so you can assign tasks separately and check progress whenever you

00:03:54.729 --> 00:03:55.009
want.

00:03:55.569 --> 00:03:59.909
You can name the teammates and spell out their roles, which keeps a long project from

00:03:59.909 --> 00:04:01.629
turning into a messy group text.

00:04:02.029 --> 00:04:06.929
It feels like study partners who each own one slice of the work, so nothing quietly falls

00:04:06.929 --> 00:04:07.669
off the table.

00:04:07.669 --> 00:04:12.749
If you are drafting a report or planning a game night, you are not juggling three separate

00:04:12.749 --> 00:04:13.489
chats anymore.

00:04:14.029 --> 00:04:19.009
Go to the chat GPT website, start a new chat, and look for the option to add team members

00:04:19.009 --> 00:04:20.169
or assign tasks.

00:04:20.729 --> 00:04:26.209
Try typing this, create a research teammate, a writing teammate, and a fact checker teammate.

00:04:26.709 --> 00:04:29.589
Then have them plan a short presentation on your favorite game.

00:04:30.229 --> 00:04:34.829
Watch the progress updates as each teammate finishes its part and hands the next step along.

00:04:34.829 --> 00:04:43.432
A modern built a different kind of teammate for games like Grand Theft Auto V or GTA V and shared the demo in a reddit post.

00:04:43.932 --> 00:04:48.012
Characters can listen to what you say and answer in their own voice in about a second.

00:04:48.492 --> 00:04:53.132
They even try to act on your words, so a quiet world starts to feel like it is paying attention.

00:04:53.812 --> 00:05:01.112
The setup uses Nvidia Nemotron for speech recognition, which means it turns your spoken words into text a computer can read.

00:05:01.112 --> 00:05:05.192
A model called QN-thate27b writes the replies.

00:05:05.772 --> 00:05:10.812
Fish audio drama 3 clones the voices, which means it copies how a character already sounds.

00:05:11.392 --> 00:05:15.472
It is still early, but it shows how a single player game can start to feel like talking

00:05:15.472 --> 00:05:16.052
with people.

00:05:16.492 --> 00:05:21.072
You can watch the short demo video from that Reddit post to see a character actually answer.

00:05:21.712 --> 00:05:26.232
The skills are packed into one downloadable package, so someone can test it on their own

00:05:26.232 --> 00:05:27.932
machine without writing new code.

00:05:27.932 --> 00:05:33.542
Joseph Gordon Levitt told Tom's Guide that the real AI worry is not killer robots

00:05:33.542 --> 00:05:34.242
from movies.

00:05:34.682 --> 00:05:39.142
He says the bigger risk is how big tech firms choose to build and control these tools.

00:05:39.742 --> 00:05:44.882
Focusing only on sci-fi machines, he argues, misses the quieter choices companies are making

00:05:44.882 --> 00:05:45.382
right now.

00:05:46.398 --> 00:05:51.418
A bioengineer report found many people dealing with cancer quietly ask AI chatbots for

00:05:51.418 --> 00:05:52.518
information or support.

00:05:53.238 --> 00:05:57.738
Chatbots here means the same kind of chat tools you might already use for homework or random

00:05:57.738 --> 00:06:03.098
questions. Patients wanted quick answers about symptoms or treatment side effects, yet

00:06:03.098 --> 00:06:07.778
most kept those chats private from their doctors, which is why checking medical advice with

00:06:07.778 --> 00:06:13.098
real professionals still matters. That's it for today. Remember, every AI expert started

